<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">mid-1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Periplus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Periplus of the Erythraean Sea is a Greek guide to navigation and trade in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, written in the mid-1st century CE. It describes ports and goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It contains the earliest historical mention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a> and its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoskales">Zoskales</a>, and documents the trade connecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> to India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea.jpg" alt="Periplus of the Erythraean Sea" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Periplus_of_the_Erythraean_Sea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
